[38;2;124;124;124minner product. Let me think about this carefully.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mAn invariant inner product on a Lie algebra $\mathfrak{g}$ is a symmetric [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mbilinear form $B$ that is non-degenerate and satisfies $B([X,Y],Z) = B(X,[Y,Z])$[0m[38;2;124;124;124mfor all $X,Y,Z \in \mathfrak{g}$ (ad-invariance).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mA Lie algebra is semisimple if and only if its Killing form is non-degenerate [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(Cartan's criterion). For a semisimple Lie algebra, the Killing form is an [0m
[38;2;124;124;124minvariant inner product.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe question: can a non-semisimple Lie algebra have an invariant inner product?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mYes. Consider a reductive Lie algebra that is not semisimple. A reductive Lie [0m
[38;2;124;124;124malgebra is the direct sum of a semisimple Lie algebra and an abelian Lie [0m
[38;2;124;124;124malgebra: $\mathfrak{g} = \mathfrak{s} \oplus \mathfrak{a}$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor example, $\mathfrak{gl}(n, \mathbb{R}) = \mathfrak{sl}(n, \mathbb{R}) \oplus[0m[38;2;124;124;124m\mathbb{R} \cdot I$. This is reductive but not semisimple (it has a nontrivial [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcenter / abelian summand).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWe can define an invariant inner product on $\mathfrak{gl}(n,\mathbb{R})$ by [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$B(X,Y) = \text{tr}(XY)$. This is the trace form. Let's verify it's non-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mdegenerate and invariant.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m- Symmetry: $\text{tr}(XY) = \text{tr}(YX)$, so symmetric. ✓[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- Non-degeneracy: if $\text{tr}(XY) = 0$ for all $Y$, then $X = 0$. This is true[0m[38;2;124;124;124mbecause the trace form on $\mathfrak{gl}(n)$ is non-degenerate (pick $Y = [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mE_{ij}$, then $\text{tr}(XE_{ij}) = X_{ji}$, so all entries of $X$ are zero). ✓[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- Invariance: $B([X,Y],Z) = \text{tr}((XY-YX)Z) = \text{tr}(XYZ) - \text{tr}[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(YXZ) = \text{tr}(X(YZ)) - \text{tr}(X(ZY)) = \text{tr}(X[Y,Z]) = B(X,[Y,Z])$. ✓[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mSo $\mathfrak{gl}(n,\mathbb{R})$ is not semisimple (it has a nontrivial center [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mconsisting of scalar matrices, and $\mathfrak{gl}(n,\mathbb{R}) = \mathfrak{sl}[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(n,\mathbb{R}) \oplus \mathbb{R}I$), yet it has an invariant inner product [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$B(X,Y) = \text{tr}(XY)$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mTherefore the answer is **Yes**.[0m

